#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# orthogonalize

#% text
# `orthogonalize(vs)` gives an orthonormal basis of the span of the vectors `vs` by the Gram-Schmidt process.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Vectors that depend on earlier ones are dropped.

#% item
# The inner product is `a . conjugate(b)`; `orthogonalize(vs, f)` uses `f(a, b)`.

#% item
# Exact input gives exact results; machine numbers are compared to zero with a relative tolerance of 1e-10.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
orthogonalize([[1, 0, 1], [1, 1, 1]])
#% output
#= [[(1/2)*2^(1/2), 0, (1/2)*2^(1/2)], [0, 1, 0]]

#% cell
orthogonalize([[2, 3], [0, 2], [3, 1]])
#% output
#= [[(2/13)*13^(1/2), (3/13)*13^(1/2)], [(-3/13)*13^(1/2), (2/13)*13^(1/2)]]

#% cell
orthogonalize([[1, 2], [2, 4]])
#% output
#= [[(1/5)*5^(1/2), (2/5)*5^(1/2)]]

#% cell
orthogonalize([[1.25, 3], [7.9, -1.4]])
#% output
#= [[0.38461538461538464, 0.9230769230769231], [0.923076923076923, -0.3846153846153846]]

